    24	<p class="kicker">Essay 3 of 7 · A narrow machine-vision product for food packaging</p>
    25	<h1>Inspect the date, not “food”</h1>
    26	<p class="lede">The sale is not artificial intelligence. It is a stainless cell that rejects a sliced-cheese pack when the thermal-transfer best-before or lot code is missing, unreadable or different from the line recipe. The plant operations manager buys after a code escape, retailer corrective action or line change; a Dutch integrator installs and supports it. That boundary makes successive deliveries similar. It also puts the idea between formidable substitutes: printer makers already join code and camera, and local vision integrators already join camera, PLC, reject and dashboard. The opportunity earns a test only if a partner will resell a validated cheese-specific module without taking all the margin and learning.</p>
    27	
    28	<section class="proposition">
    29	<h2>The proposition</h2>
    30	<p>Build one thing: an end-of-line verifier for <strong>black thermal-transfer best-before and lot codes on the glossy top film of single-file sliced-cheese thermoform packs</strong>. It sits after cutting and singulation, while the food is sealed. A monochrome camera reads the two fields, compares them with the expected strings supplied by the production recipe, and sends a pack identity and pass/fail result to the line PLC. A confirmed reject removes a failure into a locked bin. The cell stores the image, expected value, decoded value, recipe revision, decision and reject confirmation.</p>
    31	<p>The buyer is the plant operations manager at a private-label cheese slicer or co-packer. Quality writes the acceptance criteria; engineering and IT approve the connection; operations owns the downtime and signs. The purchase event is not “interest in AI.” It is a documented wrong-date or missing-code escape that requires corrective action, or a printer, labeler or line replacement that creates a funded change window.</p>
    32	<p>This business refuses five adjacent jobs: seal contamination, wrong-film artwork, barcode grading, foreign bodies and cheese defects. It does not supply a universal vision platform, rewrite the plant’s MES, act as the machine’s conformity assessor or promise that a camera prevents recalls. Each refusal preserves a repeatable field of view, defect vocabulary and acceptance test. If a buyer needs all five inspections, PackCheck, Vision Partners or a broad integrator is the honest answer.</p>
    33	<p>The default substitute is the operator’s start-up sample plus periodic quality checks. The stronger substitute is a vision option from the printer vendor or existing line integrator. The proposed company therefore sells neither labour nor components. It sells a pre-engineered optical/mechanical module, a demanding validation pack, and continuing evidence that the recipe, optics and reject path remain in control.</p>
    34	</section>
    35	
    36	<section class="changed">
    37	<h2>What changed</h2>
    38	<p>The obligation is old, not an approaching regulatory wave. Dutch NVWA guidance says durability information must be readable and findable; highly perishable foods use a use-by date and other foods a minimum-durability or best-before date. <!-- CHECK: 03-date-duty --> EU rules separately require a lot indication for food, with specified exemptions, and place it on the prepackaging or attached label when food is prepacked. <!-- CHECK: 03-lot-duty --> General Food Law requires traceability systems and requires a food business operator that believes food is unsafe to initiate withdrawal procedures once it has left the operator’s immediate control. <!-- CHECK: 03-trace-withdraw --> None of those texts says “buy machine vision.” They establish the consequence and leave the control method to the operator.</p>
    39	<p>The failure is real. In December 2025 the NVWA published a safety warning after one retailer sold pita packs without a durability date and wraps printed with 5 December 2026 instead of 5 December 2025. <!-- CHECK: 03-real-warning --> That is evidence that missing and wrong dates can pass through production and reach consumers. It is not evidence that Dutch cheese packers will spend €52,000 on this proposed cell. <!-- CHECK: 03-no-budget-proof --> Payment has to be discovered.</p>
    40	<p>The practical change is that the component stack has become product-like. Cognex currently presents In-Sight 3800 as able to read food-and-beverage lot and expiry codes on uneven surfaces; its documentation lists global-shutter monochrome sensors, discrete I/O, Ethernet and IP67 protection when correctly assembled. <!-- CHECK: 03-cognex-ocr --><!-- CHECK: 03-cognex-spec --> Domino’s R-Series packages code validation with preconfigured cameras and lights, while Videojet-Laetus combines OCR/OCV, printer synchronisation and SKU control. <!-- CHECK: 03-domino-range --><!-- CHECK: 03-videojet-system --> These are not proof that the proposed cheese module wins. They mean the founder need not invent OCR—and cannot claim OCR as a moat.</p>
    41	<p>Buyer pain, legal duty and budget remain separate. A plant may accept manual checks because escapes are rare, buy a printer-integrated camera during the next replacement, or ask its incumbent integrator to add a smart camera. The first paid diagnostic must reveal the frequency and cost of holds, rework, retailer claims and code-related nonconformities at one line. Without those numbers, the regulation is merely atmosphere.</p>
    42	</section>
    43	
    44	<section class="sale">
    45	<h2>The first sale</h2>
    46	<p>The first offer is <strong>CodeProof, a fixed €3,500 feasibility and loss diagnostic</strong>. This is a required test price, not an observed market price. Over one production shift and the following cleaning window, the work records pack sizes and spacing, line speed, printer and ribbon, code zones, recipes, PLC family, network policy, available reject length, reject-bin practice and every manual check. The customer supplies 500 representative packs and deliberately bad samples. Bench work compares two lighting geometries and returns a short specification: defect classes, optical layout, I/O map, reject concept, sanitation boundary, validation sample, downtime estimate and fixed cell quotation. It also returns “do not automate” when the defect rate and consequence cannot support the cell.</p>
    47	<p>The diagnostic is not credited against the build. Crediting turns evidence into free presales engineering. Its purchaser receives a useful line-control specification even if another integrator builds it. The operations manager signs; the quality manager owns the defect labels; the partner integrator attends the line survey because it will later own mechanics, PLC, safety and first-line support.</p>
    48	<p>The initial screen contains ten names: Royal A-ware, Vergeer Holland, Vandersterre, CONO Kaasmakers, FrieslandCampina, Royal Lactalis Leerdammer, Bouter Cheese, Kaas-Pack Holland, Henri Willig and Vepo Cheese. This is an outreach list, not a claim that any has an unprotected matching line or buying intent. <!-- CHECK: 03-prospect-list --> A partner first checks public pack format and its own installed-base knowledge, then asks the plant operations manager one question: “Show us the last code-related hold, customer complaint or audit action, and what it cost.” Five conversations with no dated event end the segment test early.</p>
    49	<p>Acquisition should begin through one Dutch packaging or vision integrator, not by the English-speaking founder cold-calling factory floors. The first named channel test is Vision Partners: ask whether it will put a white-label cheese-code module inside EVIS while retaining the mechanics, PLC and customer scope. EVIS’s published integrations make the overlap—and therefore the risk of a quick no—explicit. <!-- CHECK: 03-evis-channel --> The partner owns the Dutch introduction and safety conversation; the founder can conduct the technical workshop in English and build B1/B2 Dutch for commissioning. Zaandam is useful for travel, not a sales advantage. A business that still needs the founder to interpret rapid Dutch operator discussion at customer twenty has failed its own scale test.</p>
    50	</section>
    51	
    52	<section class="economics">
    53	<h2>Does the money work?</h2>
    54	<p>No comparable Dutch turnkey price was found in public documentation, so every number below is a hurdle. The customer price must survive a quote against Domino, Videojet-Laetus and local integrators; the company must survive after the channel has been paid. The model assumes the integrator owns the customer contract for mechanics, PLC, machinery conformity and first-line service, while the new company invoices the integrator for the optical module, software, validation and second-line support.</p>
